<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maharana Pratap</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1540–1597</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Pratap Singh I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> Pratap was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> ruler of Mewar from 1572 to 1597. He refused to submit to the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> and fought a Mughal army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i">Man Singh I</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-haldighati">Battle of Haldighati</a> in 1576. Though defeated, he continued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance from the hills and recovered much of Mewar before his death, becoming a lasting symbol of Rajput independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap.jpg" alt="Maharana Pratap" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Surendra Singh Shaktawat (Semliyaheera) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharana_Pratap_By_Surendra_Singh_Shaktawat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
